script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'p1-report',
  description: 'P1 (records v3) report: diagnose failures and timeouts per arm, write the report text, independently check it',
  phases: [{ title: 'Diagnose' }, { title: 'Report' }, { title: 'Check' }],
}
const COMMON = `Repository: /home/user/workflow-foundry. AGENTS.md rules apply. Do NOT touch git state. Never edit src/, web/, bin/, schemas/, policy/, skills/, beds/, experiments/ (frozen or owned by other agents). IMPORTANT: subagents may not create report files in this environment; return document text in your final answer when asked, and write only the scratch files named below.
Context: P1 (pre-registration research/core-20261006/PREREG.md, frozen at commit 880c6b9 with the amendments listed there) has finished: 288/288 trials in experiments/runs/p1/results.jsonl; logs experiments/runs/p1/run*.log (there were restarts: a container restart, an owner stop 23:17 -> 07:09 the next day); journal experiments/runs/p1/journal.sqlite (every model call: request, response, usage, latency, decoding); work dirs /var/tmp/foundry-work/p1/; analysis: node experiments/p1/analyze.mjs experiments/runs/p1/results.jsonl (output saved at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-workflow-foundry/d3c9fb19-46ab-5a0c-bfdd-4f8b7871088a/scratchpad/p1-analyze.txt). Known: MODEL_TIMEOUT errors on Haiku 5.5 single calls (b0call-haiku-med 11 at d4, b0call-haiku-none 4 at d4, b0ncall-haiku-med 14 at d4 + 1 at d3); the CLI provider timeout default is in src/models/providers/claude-cli.mjs and the arm code in experiments/lib/arms/b0-call.mjs. Test seeds 5001-5006 (d3, d4) are evaluation tasks; after this diagnosis they count as INSPECTED (record it).`
phase('Diagnose')
const diags = await parallel([
  () => agent(`${COMMON}
TASK: diagnose the TIMEOUTS and failures of the single-call arms (b0call-haiku-none, b0call-haiku-med, b0ncall-haiku-med, b0call-haiku45-none, b0call-sonnet-med). For timeouts: what was the timeout value, how long did the calls run, how many output tokens were being produced (journal usage/partial data if any), would the output plausibly have finished (size of the expected answer at d4 vs d3: number of records), and is the timeout a property of single calls (huge structured output in one call) or a harness choice that handicaps the baseline? Was the timeout pre-registered (check PREREG.md and the arm code)? For non-timeout failures: oracle reason classes (oracleDetails) per arm; for Haiku 4.5 (0/36) what exactly fails (format rule 'row-in-multiple-records' etc.); for Sonnet what fails. Report counts with evidence; write notes to /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-workflow-foundry/d3c9fb19-46ab-5a0c-bfdd-4f8b7871088a/scratchpad/p1-diag-single.md and return them.`, { label: 'diagnose:single-calls', phase: 'Diagnose', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' }),
  () => agent(`${COMMON}
TASK: diagnose (a) the one fhand-haiku failure and every escalation in fhand-haiku (which node escalated M->L, why, and whether the run verdict was honest: did Foundry's own verdict say pass on a failed trial?), (b) fhand-sonnet's cost profile vs fhand-haiku (which nodes cost what; output-token share), (c) b0agent-haiku's 7 failures (agent transcripts in the work dirs if kept; oracle reasons), and (d) cost accounting integrity: any costUnknown, imputed spend, trials killed by the restarts (compare run logs vs results), retries under envFaultRule. Write notes to /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-workflow-foundry/d3c9fb19-46ab-5a0c-bfdd-4f8b7871088a/scratchpad/p1-diag-workflow.md and return them.`, { label: 'diagnose:workflow-agent', phase: 'Diagnose', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' }),
])
phase('Report')
const report = await agent(`${COMMON}
TASK: write the P1 report as Markdown and RETURN THE FULL TEXT as your final answer (do not write it to the repo; the orchestrator will save it as research/core-20261006/REPORT.md). Contents: (1) what ran, exactly as pre-registered, freeze reference, deviations (restarts, any trials rerun under envFaultRule, the thinking-control probe amendment); (2) the analyze.mjs tables verbatim (re-run it); (3) the pre-registered decision rules applied mechanically: is 'cheap workflow reaches strong-single-call success at lower cost' supported (directionally)? state the exact numbers and CIs; gaps under 3 points are noise; no K=1 claims; power section (12 tasks, under-powered for success differences below ~35 points); (4) the timeout issue: how it affects each comparison; report the comparisons also restricted to d3 (where there are no single-call timeouts except one) as a sensitivity analysis clearly labelled as post-hoc; (5) failure diagnoses per arm (from below); (6) what the result does and does not mean, in plain language: a hand-written ceiling workflow on one synthetic development family; Haiku 4.5 0/36 vs Haiku 5.5; implications for C1, the router, and the wild evaluation ladder; (7) costs from the ledgers; (8) inspected seeds 5001-5006 at d3/d4 must never be reused as held-out. Mark claims measured/documented/inferred.
DIAGNOSES:
${diags.filter(Boolean).join('\n\n----\n\n')}`, { label: 'report:p1', phase: 'Report', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' })
phase('Check')
const check = await agent(`${COMMON}
TASK: independently CHECK this P1 report draft against the raw data: re-run the analysis, recount every number from results.jsonl, verify each diagnosis claim on at least two trials against the journal, hunt for overclaims (beyond the pre-registered decision rules; post-hoc analyses not labelled; development evidence presented as general; the timeout handicap understated or overstated), and missing caveats. Return (1) a list of required corrections, each as an exact old-text -> new-text replacement against the draft, and (2) any caveats to append. Do not write files.
DRAFT:
${report}`, { label: 'check:p1', phase: 'Check', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' })
return { report, check }
